    "factsheet": [
        {
            "heading": "Brief History",
            "paragraphs": [
                "The building that became Worlds End Studios originally stood on the site of a former factory connected to Chelsea’s flour mills, where machinery for baking ovens was made. After a fire destroyed the factory, the derelict structure remained abandoned until two architects bought it from the council in 1974 and converted it into a creative workspace. They preserved much of the original industrial structure, creating the collaborative studio complex that still exists today."
            ]
        }
    ],
